-### Supersampling Baseline
-A baseline method for evaluating NeRF on multiscale data by training on full-resolution images and using brute-force supersampling during evaluation, or training on multiscale data with supersampling during both training and evaluation [S21]. For every pixel, multiple jittered rays are sampled uniformly at random through the spatial footprint of the pixel. The mean of the rendered values is used as the predicted color in the loss function [S21]. This baseline relies on 'oracle' scale information that does not exist for most datasets [S21]. The 16x supersampling baseline performs worse than mip-NeRF and is significantly more expensive during both training and evaluation [S21].
-
-### Two-stage training for proposers
-A training schedule split into two equal-duration phases to stabilize learning of a trainable proposer network in NeRF-ID [S22, unofficial]. Phase 1 trains the learnt proposer to mimic the heuristic proposer by minimizing the L2 matching loss between their proposal sets [S22, unofficial]. Phase 2 trains all network components end-to-end, resetting the optimizer state and performing a linear warmup for 1,000 steps to reduce shock from switching regimes [S22, unofficial]. Training from scratch sometimes works but often fails; two-stage training is consistently effective [S22, unofficial].
-
-### Surface-aware density formulation
-A method to extract high-quality surfaces from NeRF by modeling geometry as a distance function or occupancy field and converting its output to density using a probability density function [S23]. It incorporates an eikonal term to encourage geometric consistency, enforcing that the gradient norm of the learned SDF is equal to one [S23]. The density distribution is modeled as $\Phi_S(x) = \frac{se^{-sx}}{1+e^{-sx}}$, where $s$ is a scaling parameter and $x$ is the SDF or occupancy value [S23].
-
-### RapNeRF
-A NeRF model tailored for view extrapolation, contrasting with standard NeRFs that excel at interpolation [S24]. Training begins with a baseline NeRF to recover a rough mesh. A Ray Atlas (RA) is created by extracting a rough 3D mesh from a pretrained NeRF and mapping training ray directions onto its vertices [S24]. Random Ray Casting (RRC) is employed: for a training ray hitting a surface point $v = o + tz d$, a backward ray is cast from $v$ toward a new origin $o'$ with uniformly sampled angular perturbations [S24]. RRC and RA augment training rays with a predetermined probability [S24].
-
-### JaxNeRF
-A re-implementation of the original NeRF in JAX, used as a benchmark comparison for early works seeking to improve on training and rendering speed [S24]. It is slightly faster and more suited for distributed computing than the original TensorFlow implementation of NeRF [S24].
-
-### SNeS
-A method improving geometry by learning probable symmetries for partly symmetric and partly hidden in-scene objects through soft symmetry constraints on geometry and material properties [S24].
-
-### DreamAvatar
-A diffusion-based 3D avatar model generation method with text guidance, using an SMPL model as a shape prior and text-guided 2D image generation via diffusion to create training data in a DreamFusion-like 3D generation pipeline combining NeRF and diffusion [S24].
-
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$, where $D$ is the MLP dimensionality. The dominant cost is the neural network query per sample point [S5, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot D)$ for the forward pass activations, plus $O(N_{iters})$ for optimizer states (Adam stores two moments per parameter) [S5, 44-radiance-field-optimization.md, unofficial]. Chunking reduces peak memory usage during the forward pass [S5, S7, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Training typically requires 100,000 to 1,000,000 iterations [S11, S14, unofficial]. On standard datasets (Blender, LLFF), convergence is observed within this range [S11, S14, unofficial]. Batch sizes of 1024-2048 rays are common [S5, S11, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with the number of training views and image resolution. Larger batch sizes improve throughput but may hinder data loading performance and GPU occupancy if too large; smaller batch sizes cap iterations per second [S15, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; leverages standard deep learning frameworks; produces high-quality novel views for interpolation [S5, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Slow training due to repeated MLP queries; prone to overfitting on sparse views without regularization; struggles with view extrapolation [S1, S24].
-*   **When to Use:** Standard novel view synthesis from dense multi-view images.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time training is required, or when dealing with extreme sparsity without additional priors/regularization.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot K)$, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $N_{rand}$ is the batch size (rays per step), and $K$ is the number of samples per ray [S5, S14, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot K)$ for storing intermediate rendering states per batch, plus the memory for the neural network weights [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. Chunking reduces peak VRAM usage by processing rays in smaller subsets [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Standard NeRF training on Blender datasets (800x800) typically requires ~100,000 iterations, taking several hours on a single GPU [S11, S14, unofficial]. Batch sizes of 1024–2048 are common trade-offs between data loading performance and GPU occupancy [S15, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** End-to-end differentiable; handles complex geometry and appearance; robust to varying lighting conditions when trained on multi-view images.
+*   **Limitations:** Slow convergence due to the need for many iterations; high memory usage for large scenes or high resolutions; prone to "floaters" and transparency artifacts in unobserved regions [S1][S17, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When high-fidelity novel view synthesis from multi-view images is required and computational resources allow for long training times.
+*   **When Not to Use:** For real-time applications or when training data is extremely sparse without additional regularization/priors [S1, S23].
 
 ## Background
-The NeRF Training Loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S5, unofficial][S20]. It builds on the concept of implicit neural representations for 3D scenes [S3, unofficial][S20]. The method uses a coordinate-based MLP to map 3D positions and viewing directions to volume density and color, optimized via stochastic gradient descent on pixel-wise MSE loss [S5, S14, unofficial]. It has become a foundational technique in 3D computer vision, inspiring numerous variants for acceleration, sparse views, and dynamic scenes [S1, S24].
+The NeRF Training Loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S5, unofficial]. It established the standard protocol for optimizing coordinate-based MLPs to represent continuous 3D scenes. The method builds on the differentiable volume rendering equation and uses stochastic gradient descent with Adam to minimize the pixel-wise MSE loss. Subsequent works have extended this loop with coarse-to-fine sampling [S5, unofficial], positional encoding [S20], and various regularization techniques [S7, unofficial][S8].
 
